After installation:

rebooted the VM
removed the Ubuntu ISO from UTM
booted successfully into the installed Ubuntu system


Step 5 — Open Terminal and Practice Linux Commands

I opened the Ubuntu terminal and practiced basic Linux commands to learn how to navigate the Linux filesystem and manage files and folders.

First, I used the pwd command to display the current working directory. Then I used the ls command to list files and folders in the current directory.

Next, I created my first folder called “Practice Folder” using the mkdir command. After creating the folder, I navigated into it using the cd command.

Inside the folder, I created my first text file called notes.txt using the touch command. Finally, I used ls again to verify that the file was successfully created.

This step introduced me to:

Linux terminal navigation
file and folder management
basic Linux commands
working with directories in a Linux environment


Step 6 — Find Ubuntu VM IP Address

I used the ip a command inside the Ubuntu terminal to identify the virtual machine’s IP address. The IP address assigned to the VM was 192.168.64.3.

This step helped me understand:

Linux network interfaces
local IP addressing
how virtual machines communicate on a network

Step 7 — Install OpenSSH Server

To remotely connect to the Ubuntu VM from my Mac, I installed the OpenSSH server package.

First, I updated Ubuntu’s package list using:

sudo apt update

Then I installed the OpenSSH server package using:

sudo apt install openssh-server -y

After installation, I started the SSH service and verified that the service was actively running.

This step introduced me to:

package management with apt
Linux services
remote access technologies
SSH server configuration

Step 8 — SSH Into Ubuntu VM From macOS

Using the macOS terminal, I remotely connected into the Ubuntu virtual machine using SSH.

I used the VM’s IP address and my Ubuntu username to establish the connection.

During the first connection attempt, SSH displayed a fingerprint verification message asking whether I trusted the host machine. I typed “yes” to continue and then entered my Ubuntu password.

After successful authentication, I gained full remote terminal access to the Ubuntu virtual machine directly from my Mac.

This step helped me understand:

remote Linux administration
SSH authentication
client/server communication
secure remote access







